# floor_derive_feed.cam — the K3 slice-1 acceptance fixture (DECISIONS.md →
# "K3, the material…", slice 1): both derived words on one real op, behind
# the opt-in — the sibling of floor_derive.cam, whose tool deliberately
# declares NO flute count and must keep deriving only S.
#
# The tool carries K2's flute_count and the op replaces BOTH numbers with the
# word `derive`, so the shipped 6061×Endmill row closes both identities at
# validate: RPM = 1000·150/(π·10) = 4774.648, and F = RPM × z × f_z =
# 4774.648… × 3 × 0.040 (the 6..13 band) = 572.958. The feed identity reads
# the op's RESOLVED spindle — here the derived one, since spindle=derive
# rides along per the entry's own sequencing — because the chip thins by what
# the spindle actually turns. The globals below are what every op WITHOUT the
# sentinel would still get, byte for byte.
units mm
feed 800
plunge 300
rapid_z 5
clearance_z 25
spindle 3000

stock Box(w=120, l=80, h=20, x=0, y=0, z=0, material=6061)

tool T5 Endmill(dia=10.0, flute_count=3)

# The Plane is the FINISHED face — stock top is z=0, so a 0.5mm skim names
# its face at z=-0.5 (the plate.cam stance).
feature TopFace Plane(z=-0.5)

run End(tool=T5, surface=TopFace, stepover=6.0, spindle=derive, feed=derive)
